Optimal Solution - Use a HashMap to count frequencies and return the k-th string that appears exactly once while preserving order.
                   “Since order matters, I first count frequencies using a HashMap, then scan the array again to locate the k-th string with frequency one.”

class Solution {
    public String kthDistinct(String[] arr, int k) {
        // Map to store frequency of each string
        Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        // Count frequency
        for (String str : arr) {
            freq.put(str, freq.getOrDefault(str, 0) + 1);
        }
        // Traverse in original order
        for (String str : arr) {
            // If string appears exactly once
            if (freq.get(str) == 1) {
                k--;  // Decrease k
                // If this is the kth distinct string
                if (k == 0) {
                    return str;
                }
            }
        
        // If fewer than k distinct strings
        return "";
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(N).
